---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'China to GCC Freight Calculators | Freightgcc';
const description =
  'Use free Freightgcc calculators for landed cost, chargeable weight, FCL versus LCL, DDP versus DAP, transit buffers and HS classification research.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Are the Freightgcc calculators free to use?',
    a: 'Yes. All six tools run in your browser and are free. There is no account, subscription, cookie-based tracking or email capture. You enter the shipment values, the page calculates locally, and nothing is sent to a server. The calculators are planning aids rather than commercial services, so there is no quote request and no forwarding of your data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do the calculators use live freight rates?',
    a: 'No. Freightgcc does not publish live carrier or forwarder rates, and the tools do not fetch market prices. You supply the FOB value, freight, charges and transit days from your own quotes or operating data. The results show only what follows from those inputs. A freight rate changes weekly, while the arithmetic linking it to duty, VAT and dates should remain inspectable.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which calculator should I use first?',
    a: 'Start with chargeable weight if you are comparing air and LCL quotes, because it tells you the billable quantity. Use landed cost once you have a freight figure and an HS duty rate. Use transit buffer when the commercial question is a delivery date rather than a price. The six results can be combined manually; the tools do not pass data from one to another.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use these tools outside the GCC?',
    a: 'The arithmetic is generic, but the built-in VAT presets and explanations are specific to Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Qatar, Kuwait, Oman and Bahrain. For another destination, use the formulas as a template and enter that country’s statutory rates yourself. Confirm classification and tax treatment with a local broker or customs adviser. This site does not provide customs advice.',
  },
];

const faqPage = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: faq.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={[faqPage]}>
  <h1>Freight calculators for China to GCC shipments</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    These six free planning tools turn the definitions used across Freightgcc into working
    arithmetic: landed cost, chargeable weight, FCL versus LCL, DDP versus DAP, transit buffers and an HS classification research brief. They are designed for importers,
    sourcing teams and operations staff moving goods from China to Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Qatar,
    Kuwait, Oman and Bahrain. Every commercial number comes from you; the calculators do not quote
    freight or customs charges.
  </p>

  <h2>Which calculator should I use?</h2>
  <p>
    The right tool depends on the question in front of you. If a forwarder has quoted a per-kilogram
    air rate and a per-cubic-metre LCL rate, chargeable weight tells you which quantity will be
    billed. If the question is what the shipment costs after duty, VAT and delivery, use landed cost.
    If the customer has asked for a date and you need to know how much calendar float to protect, use
    transit buffer. Each calculator is deliberately narrow so its arithmetic can be checked.
  </p>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/landed-cost/">Landed cost calculator</a></h3>
      <p>
        Combines FOB value, freight, insurance, duty, VAT and four user-entered charges into CIF,
        total outlay and net cost after recoverable VAT. Use it for a Saudi, UAE, Qatari, Kuwaiti,
        Omani or Bahraini import.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: import budgets, supplier comparisons and DDP checks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/chargeable-weight/">Chargeable weight calculator</a></h3>
      <p>
        Calculates air volumetric kilograms at the ÷6000 convention and LCL revenue tons under the
        W/M rule. It shows total volume, gross weight and which measure governs the bill.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: checking air and LCL quotes before booking.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/transit-buffer/">Transit buffer calculator</a></h3>
      <p>
        Converts departure date, ocean or air transit days, clearance days and a buffer into an
        arrival estimate, an arrival window, a latest departure date and days remaining.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: delivery promises and purchase-order deadlines.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/fcl-vs-lcl/">FCL vs LCL calculator</a></h3>
      <p>
        Compares full-container and less-than-container freight from your own rates. It shows LCL
        chargeable W/M, the container count, the break-even volume and the lower-cost option.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: choosing a mode before requesting a booking.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/ddp-vs-dap/">DDP vs DAP calculator</a></h3>
      <p>
        Separates the seller’s DDP cost floor from the buyer’s DAP import charges, using your invoice,
        freight, duty, VAT and destination figures.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: comparing incoterm quotes and import cash flow.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card tool-card">
      <h3><a href="/tools/hs-code-research/">HS Code research worksheet</a></h3>
      <p>
        Builds a broker-ready classification brief from the product, material, function and destination.
        It does not guess a code or query a tariff database.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">Best for: preparing a classification question, not replacing a ruling.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What do the six tools calculate?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Core calculation</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Landed cost</td>
          <td>What will the goods cost once GCC duty and VAT are included?</td>
          <td>Duty on CIF; VAT on CIF plus duty; extras added at the end.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Chargeable weight</td>
          <td>Which quantity will the airline or LCL consolidator bill?</td>
          <td>Air: greater of actual and volumetric kilograms. LCL: greater of tonnes and cubic metres.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transit buffer</td>
          <td>When could it arrive, and when must it leave to meet a target?</td>
          <td>Calendar-date arithmetic using transit, clearance and buffer days.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>FCL vs LCL</td>
          <td>Is a full container or LCL cheaper for this volume and weight?</td>
          <td>LCL W/M versus FCL container count at the capacity you enter.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>DDP vs DAP</td>
          <td>What does the seller include under DDP, and what does the buyer pay under DAP?</td>
          <td>DDP cost floor versus DAP buyer import charges.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>HS Code research</td>
          <td>What facts does a broker need to classify the product?</td>
          <td>A structured brief; no code lookup or guessed duty rate.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Which inputs do I need before I start?</h2>
  <p>
    For landed cost, collect the commercial invoice value, a freight quote, your insurance rate, the
    destination market, and the duty rate attached to the HS code. The destination charges,
    clearance fee and inland delivery should be your own figures. The calculator supplies only the
    country VAT preset, and that preset is a statutory rate to confirm before use. It does not supply
    a duty rate, because duty depends on classification and the tariff line.
  </p>
  <p>
    For chargeable weight, measure one carton or pallet in centimetres and weigh it in kilograms.
    Record the number of identical pieces. If the consignment contains mixed carton sizes, calculate
    each line separately and add the chargeable results. For transit buffer, use the sailing or flight
    transit quoted by your forwarder, add your broker’s expected clearance days, and choose a buffer
    that reflects the consequence of a late arrival rather than a wish for certainty.
  </p>

  <h2>Why use a range instead of a single number?</h2>
  <p>
    A calculator produces a point estimate from point inputs. In real freight planning, several
    inputs are ranges: the sailing may arrive two days early or six days late, the customs officer
    may inspect the container, and the duty line may be reviewed. The result is still useful because
    it shows the structure of the cost or schedule. Change one input at a time and the sensitivity
    becomes visible. That is more honest than presenting a single “all-in” number as if every
    assumption were settled.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the result as a base case, then test the downside. Re-run landed cost with a higher duty rate,
    re-run chargeable weight with the dimensions the carrier may use, or re-run transit buffer with a
    larger clearance allowance. The tools do not model probability, and they do not predict carrier
    behaviour. They make the consequences of different assumptions easy to compare.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the shared assumptions and limitations?</h2>
  <p>
    All six tools use arithmetic conventions explained on the individual pages. Landed cost assumes
    duty is assessed on CIF and VAT on CIF plus duty. Chargeable weight uses a 6,000 cm³/kg air
    divisor and a 1,000 kg per cubic metre LCL convention. Transit buffer uses calendar days, not
    working days. None of the tools tracks live rates, reads a customs database, converts currency,
    or checks an HS code. They are not connected to a carrier or a customs authority.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those boundaries matter. A calculator cannot tell you whether a free-zone treatment changes VAT,
    whether an inspection fee will apply, or whether a carrier rounds the chargeable weight differently.
    It cannot see blank sailings or a public holiday. Use the output to structure a conversation with
    a broker or forwarder, not to replace the documents and advice that support an import.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the disclaimer?</h2>
  <p>
    These are planning tools, not quotations, customs rulings or financial advice. Every result
    depends on the values entered. Confirm rates, classification, tax treatment and dates with the
    relevant authorities or your appointed service providers before making a commercial commitment.
  </p>

  <h2>What else should importers ask about these tools?</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    {faqs.map((faq) => (
      <>
        <dt>{faq.q}</dt>
        <dd>{faq.a}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <section class="related">
    <h2>Where should I read next?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/guides/cost-and-transit/">China–GCC freight costs and transit</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/air-freight/">Air freight to the GCC</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/lcl/">LCL to the GCC</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/ddp-vs-self-clearance/">DDP versus self-clearance</a></li>
      <li><a href="/countries/">Country guides</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
